# Monetary Policy: The RBI's Toolkit

> Monetary policy manages money, credit, and interest to hold prices
> steady while financing growth — through rates, reserves, and
> persuasion.

[definition]
**Monetary policy** is the central bank's use of instruments —
policy rates, reserve ratios, open market operations, and guidance —
to control money supply and the cost of credit in pursuit of price
stability with growth.
[/definition]

## 1. Objectives and the Inflation-Targeting Framework

Since the 2016 amendment to the RBI Act (Section 45ZA), India follows
**flexible inflation targeting**: CPI inflation of **4% with a band
of 2–6%**. A **Monetary Policy Committee (MPC)** of **six members**
— the RBI Governor as chair, the Deputy Governor in charge of
monetary policy, one RBI officer, and three government nominees —
votes by majority, meeting at least four times a year.

## 2. Quantitative Instruments

| Tool | What it is | Tightening use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Repo rate** | Rate at which the RBI lends short-term to banks against securities | Raise it — loans get costlier |
| **Reverse repo / SDF** | Rate for banks' surplus funds parked with the RBI | Raise it — banks park money instead of lending |
| **Bank rate** | Penal rate for rediscounting bills; long-standing signal rate | Raise it |
| **CRR** | Share of deposits banks must hold as cash with the RBI | Raise it — credit multiplier falls |
| **SLR** | Share of deposits in liquid assets — cash, gold, approved securities | Raise it — lendable funds fall |
| **Open market operations (OMO)** | Buying/selling government securities | Sell securities — money is absorbed |

[info title="Marginal Standing Facility (MSF)"]
Banks can borrow overnight from the RBI **above** the repo quota by
dipping into their SLR portfolio — at the MSF rate, set above repo.
**SDF (Standing Deposit Facility)**, introduced in 2022, absorbs
surplus liquidity *without collateral*, replacing the fixed-rate
reverse repo as the corridor's floor.
[/info]

The **liquidity corridor** runs from the SDF rate (floor) to the MSF
rate (ceiling), with the repo rate in between.

## 3. Qualitative (Selective) Instruments

- **Margin requirement** — raise the down-payment percentage for
  loans against sensitive commodities to cool speculative borrowing.
- **Selective credit controls** — stricter terms for lending against
  particular goods (sugar, pulses) when hoarding emerges.
- **Credit rationing** — ceilings on how much banks may lend to
  certain sectors.
- **Moral suasion** — the RBI persuades banks through letters,
  meetings, and public statements, without formal order.

## 4. The Transmission Mechanism

@diagram tree id="transmission-chain" layout="tree" aria="Monetary policy transmission chain"
  "RBI raises repo rate" -> "Banks' cost of funds rises"
  "Banks' cost of funds rises" -> "Lending rates rise"
  "Lending rates rise" -> "Credit and demand cool"
  "Credit and demand cool" -> "Inflation pressure eases"
@/diagram

Transmission is slow — a repo change typically takes a few quarters
to show fully in lending rates, spending, and prices.

## 5. Tight vs Easy Money

| Aspect | Tight (dear) money | Easy (cheap) money |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Repo, bank rate | High | Low |
| CRR / SLR | High | Low |
| OMO | Sell securities | Buy securities |
| Credit | Contracts | Expands |
| Used against | Inflation | Recession, slowdown |
| Risk | Growth and jobs slow | Inflation and asset bubbles |

[example]
Facing an inflation surge, the RBI raises the repo rate in steps;
EMIs on floating-rate home loans rise within two quarters as
external benchmark lending rates reset, households cut spending, and
demand pressure eases — tight money in action.
[/example]

## 6. Limitations of Monetary Policy

- **Time lags** — recognition, action, and effect each take months.
- **Cost-push inflation** resists rate medicine — supply shocks need
  supply-side answers.
- **Excess liquidity** from capital inflows can defeat tightening.
- **Poor transmission** if banks pass on rate changes slowly or
  weak demand caps credit offtake.

## 7. Quick Revision Table

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Target | CPI 4%, band 2–6% (2016 framework) |
| MPC | 6 members; Governor chairs; majority vote |
| Repo | RBI lends to banks against securities |
| SDF (2022) | Collateral-free absorption; corridor floor |
| CRR vs SLR | Cash with RBI vs liquid assets held by bank |
| Qualitative tools | Margins, rationing, moral suasion, selective controls |
| Tight money fights | Inflation (raise rates, sell securities) |
| Easy money fights | Recession (cut rates, buy securities) |

[summary]
The MPC targets 4% CPI inflation through a toolkit of rates (repo,
bank rate, MSF, SDF), reserves (CRR, SLR), open-market sales, and
selective controls. The chain runs from rate to bank cost to credit
to demand to prices — powerful, but slow and helpless against
supply shocks alone.
[/summary]
